    "case_id": "620",
    "case_summary": "A 59-year-old Japanese man presented with malaise and chills that began the day before his visit. Medical history included hypertension and hyperlipidemia. Four months prior, he was diagnosed with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) and had a history of perianal abscess during induction chemotherapy. The current admission was for maintenance chemotherapy with idarubicin and cytarabine. On day 10 of hospitalization, he experienced a 40.2°C fever, chills, and right upper quadrant abdominal pain when breathing deeply or coughing.\n\nVital signs:- Blood pressure: 122/74 mm Hg- Heart rate: 109 beats/min (consistently tachycardic)- Respiratory rate: 24 breaths/min- Oxygen saturation: 98% on room airPhysical findings:- Percussion tenderness in the right upper quadrant- Remainder of examination unremarkable\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Blood count: Leukocyte count 0.1 × 10⁹/L (50% neutrophils), Thrombocytes 11.0 × 10⁹/L, Hemoglobin 8.0 g/L- Liver function: Total bilirubin 2.0 mg/dL, Aspartate aminotransferase 52 U/L, Alkaline phosphatase 1065 U/L, γ-Glutamyl transpeptidase 168 U/LImaging Studies:- Image Title: Abdominal CT scan- Image Description: Multiple, small, circumscribed, hypoattenuating lesions in the liver and spleenMicrobiological Studies:- Blood cultures: Positive for yeast after 30 hours- Gram staining: Revealed arthroconidia and hyphal fungi- Culture on Sabouraud dextrose agar: Dry cottony colonies with frosted glass appearance- Molecular identification: D1/D2 26S rDNA and internal transcribed spacer region of rRNA gene analysis performed- Drug susceptibility testing: MIC values - voriconazole 0.25 μg/mL, micafungin 4.0 μg/mL",
    "frontier": [
      "B1",
      "B2",
      "B3"
    ],
